---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Performance — sandkiln">
  <Header active="performance" />

  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Real numbers from real hardware.</h1>
      <p>Every figure here came from <code>criterion</code> benchmarks or a scripted load test against a live daemon on real KVM — not a spec sheet claim. This page also covers the concrete next lever for startup latency, and why it's the right one to build next rather than a vaguer "make it faster."</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">Measured, not estimated</div>
        <h2>Cold boot, exec, and a full lifecycle under load.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="bench-grid">
        <div class="bench-card">
          <div class="label">Cold boot (criterion)</div>
          <div class="value">10.5–10.9<span style="font-size:16px;">ms</span></div>
          <div class="ctx">Process spawn to InstanceStart acknowledged — was 32.3–33.1ms before fixing a 20ms fixed-sleep poll, see below</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-card">
          <div class="label">Exec round-trip (criterion)</div>
          <div class="value">220–250<span style="font-size:16px;">µs</span></div>
          <div class="ctx">Over an already-open vsock connection</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-card">
          <div class="label">Resume from snapshot (criterion)</div>
          <div class="value">6.5–7.7<span style="font-size:16px;">ms</span></div>
          <div class="ctx">Was 25.5–26.2ms — shares the same fixed socket-wait boot fixed below</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-card">
          <div class="label">Snapshot take (criterion)</div>
          <div class="value">~322<span style="font-size:16px;">ms</span></div>
          <div class="ctx">Pause + write full memory/state to disk</div>
        </div>
        <div class="bench-card">
          <div class="label">Load test throughput</div>
          <div class="value">5.38<span style="font-size:16px;">/sec</span></div>
          <div class="ctx">4 concurrent workers × 5 cycles each, 0 errors</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-bottom: 12px;">
        <table class="lat-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Min</th><th>Mean</th><th>P95</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>create</td><td>137ms</td><td>211ms</td><td>577ms</td></tr>
            <tr><td>exec</td><td>—</td><td>366ms</td><td>—</td></tr>
            <tr><td>delete</td><td>—</td><td>131ms</td><td>—</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">A dash means the run didn't record that figure, not that it was zero. Only the numbers written down at the time appear here.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><b>What we measured:</b> cold boot via <code>criterion</code> at 10.5–10.9ms, an exec round-trip on an already-open vsock connection at 220–250µs, and a load test of 4 concurrent workers running 5 cycles each of create → exec → delete against the live daemon.</p>
        <p><b>What we found:</b> the load test's <code>create</code> phase had a mean of 369ms (p95 588ms) — far above the ~33ms cold-boot figure sitting right next to it. The gap traced to the ~300MB rootfs copy, run synchronously right after the network lease, dominating the whole phase even though boot itself was fast.</p>
        <p><b>What we fixed:</b> the rootfs copy and the network lease are independent work with no reason to serialize — they now run concurrently on separate threads — and the copy itself switched to <code>cp --reflink=auto</code>, an instant copy-on-write clone on a filesystem that supports it. Re-measured: <code>create</code> mean dropped from 369ms to 211ms, a real, repeatable win.</p>
        <p><b>What we found next, from a full per-phase profiling pass:</b> 20 isolated cold creates broke <b>167.65ms</b> down completely (0.32ms unaccounted) — the rootfs clone was <b>74.0%</b> of it (124.09ms), boot itself <b>20.3%</b> (34.00ms), and a synchronous history-store write <b>5.5%</b> (9.24ms). The network lease this project had suspected — three <code>ip</code>/<code>bridge</code> subprocess calls — turned out to be <b>~4.36ms</b>, not the bottleneck at all.</p>
        <p><b>What we fixed from that:</b> 20.11ms of every single boot (min 20.04, max 20.18 — the signature of one quantized sleep, not real waiting) was a fixed <code>sleep(20ms)</code> polling for Firecracker's API socket to exist. Replaced with a real connect-retry on a 200µs→5ms backoff — which also closes a genuine race the file-existence poll had (the socket file appears at <code>bind()</code>, a moment before <code>listen()</code>). Re-measured: boot <b>34.00ms → 11.22/11.44ms</b>, cold create <b>167.65ms → 144.59/143.56ms</b> — a real <b>~14%</b> cut to every cold create, and to snapshot resume too, which shares the same code path.</p>
        <p><b>A correction, made honestly:</b> this page previously concluded the remaining gap "needs a CoW-capable filesystem or a device-mapper layer" — tested live on a real XFS filesystem, which confirmed a genuine copy-on-write clone but changed <i>nothing</i> about end-to-end create latency. The explanation offered at the time (the copy was already hidden behind the network lease) turns out to be wrong — the lease is only ~4ms. The leading replacement explanation, <b>not yet re-verified</b>: the per-sandbox rootfs clone's destination is always <code>/tmp</code> (ext4), regardless of where the base image lives, so a cross-filesystem <code>--reflink=auto</code> could never have actually reflinked even when the source was on XFS. See <a href="../docs/architecture/engineering-notebook/">the engineering notebook</a> for the full story, including the mistake in our own earlier measurement.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">Shipped: pre-warmed pools</div>
        <h2>The mechanism already existed. Now it's proactive.</h2>
        <p>Firecracker's own published numbers document sub-second, even sub-125ms, boot times and under-5MiB per-VM memory overhead — sandkiln's own measured 32.3–33.1ms cold boot is consistent with that. Production Firecracker users solve cold-start latency the same way: not booting from scratch at all, but snapshotting a VM that's already past initialization and restoring that instead. sandkiln had the primitive (<code>snapshot</code>/<code>resume</code>/<code>fork</code>, auto-suspend) for a while before it had the proactive half — a <b><code>POST /pools</code></b> configures a pool, a background replenisher keeps a target number of resumable snapshots ready per pool, and a plain <code>Sandbox.create()</code> matching one resumes it automatically instead of cold-booting. No special call — <code>Pool.create/list/delete</code> configures it, everything else about creating a sandbox stays the same.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-block">
        <span class="tag">What we measured</span>
        <h3>A real, modest win on a clean claim — not a clean, unconditional one</h3>
        <p>A claim that resumes cleanly measured at roughly <b>70–200ms</b> against a cold create's own <b>~160–200ms</b> on the same box — a real, repeatable win at this sample size, consistent with resume's own ~25.8ms criterion number sitting well under cold boot's ~32ms. But that clean case is <b>not</b> the reliable common case on this dev box today — see the finding below, which is the more important result this work actually produced.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-block">
        <span class="tag">What we found — the more significant result</span>
        <h3>Resuming a snapshot has a real, non-rare failure mode</h3>
        <p>Building a pool means resuming far more often than any prior manual test ever had, and that surfaced something real: a resumed guest kernel can panic early in boot (an early-boot divide-by-zero trap in the console driver, caught via Firecracker's own captured console log — built specifically so a guest crash before vsock comes up isn't invisible). Measured directly, repeatedly, across clean isolated test runs: the failure rate ranged from roughly <b>1 in 3 to 2 in 3</b> resumes — not a rare edge case. The existing snapshot/resume integration check still passes every run, because one resume in a row isn't enough attempts to reliably hit it.</p>
        <p>Root cause is still an open question — plausibly TSC/clock-source drift between snapshot-time and restore-time, but that's an informed guess, not a confirmed diagnosis; see <a href="../docs/architecture/engineering-notebook/">the engineering notebook</a> for the full investigation. Handled either way: every claim runs a real post-resume health check before being handed to a caller, and a failed check tears the sandbox down and falls back to a normal cold create — a caller never receives a dead sandbox id, confirmed via repeated stress tests showing zero caller-visible failures across every run.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="section-head" style="margin-top:48px;">
        <div class="eyebrow">What's next</div>
        <h2>The remaining latency gap, and where it actually lives.</h2>
        <p>A complete phase breakdown of a 144ms cold create (post-fix), from 20 isolated, controlled measurements:</p>
      </div>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="margin-bottom: 24px;">
        <table class="lat-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Share of a cold create</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>rootfs clone (<code>cp --reflink=auto</code>)</td><td>74.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Vm::boot total</td><td>20.3%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>— wait for the API socket (now fixed, was 12.0%)</td><td>0.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>— InstanceStart</td><td>7.1%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>— configuration PUTs (all 7 combined)</td><td>1.0%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>history-store write</td><td>5.5%</td></tr>
            <tr><td>network lease (fully concurrent with the clone)</td><td>~0.1%</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="note-block">
        <span class="tag">The concrete next lever, if the reflink hypothesis holds</span>
        <h3>Give the per-sandbox rootfs clone a configurable destination</h3>
        <p>The rootfs clone is <b>74% of a cold create</b> — genuinely the dominant cost, confirmed by direct measurement, not assumption. Whether a CoW-capable filesystem actually closes that gap is <b>not yet re-verified</b> (see the correction above) — the clone always lands in <code>std::env::temp_dir()</code> regardless of where the base image lives, so today's config has no way to put both on the same filesystem to test cleanly. That's the next real step: a configurable clone destination, then a clean re-measurement — not another filesystem swap without fixing the destination first.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="note-block">
        <span class="tag">Ideas — not shipped, not verified</span>
        <h3>A few more directions worth a real look</h3>
        <p>Flagged explicitly as brainstorming, not results, per this project's own standard of never stating an unverified claim as fact:</p>
        <p><b>Whether jailer's chroot hard-linking adds measurable overhead.</b> Jailer boots via a chroot with hard-linked kernel/rootfs/drive copies rather than the direct paths a non-jailed boot uses — nobody has benchmarked whether that link step itself is visible next to boot time, since jailer hasn't been proven on real hardware yet at all (see the Roadmap page).</p>
        <p><b>Moving the history-store write off the create critical path.</b> Measured at 9.24ms (5.5% of a create) for a write its own code already treats as best-effort. Looks like an easy, real win — not yet attempted or measured as a change.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
